{"name":"Black Dahlia","hex":"#4e434d","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"black","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Absorbing all light and reflecting none; dark and hueless.","Without light."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour/color perceived in the absence of light, but also when no light is reflected, but rather absorbed.","A black dye or pigment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/black"},{"term":"dahlia","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any plant of the genus Dahlia, tuberous perennial flowering plants native to Mexico."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dahlia"}],"entries":[{"title":"Black Dahlia","blocks":[{"text":"Elizabeth Short, posthumously known as the Black Dahlia, was an American woman found murdered in the Leimert Park neighborhood of Los Angeles, California, on January 15, 1947. Her case became highly publicized owing to the gruesome nature of the crime, which included the mutilation and bisection of her corpse."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Elizabeth_Short_photo_from_police_bulletin.jpg/330px-Elizabeth_Short_photo_from_police_bulletin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Dahlia","wikidata":"Q233693"},{"title":"Black Dahlia (video game)","blocks":[{"text":"Black Dahlia is an interactive movie point-and-click adventure game that was released on February 25, 1998 by Take-Two Interactive. It is the last of three full-motion video adventure games developed by Take-Two, the other two being Hell: A Cyberpunk Thriller and Ripper."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/54/Black_Dahlia_Coverart.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Dahlia_(video_game)","wikidata":"Q4920619"},{"title":"Dahlia","blocks":[{"text":"Dahlia is a genus of bushy, tuberous, herbaceous perennial plants native to Mexico and Central America. Dahlias are members of the Asteraceae family of dicotyledonous plants, its relatives include the sunflower, daisy, chrysanthemum, and zinnia. There are 49 species of dahlia, with flowers in almost every hue, with hybrids commonly grown as garden plants."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Dahlia_x_hybrida.jpg/330px-Dahlia_x_hybrida.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dahlia","wikidata":"Q130918"},{"title":"Black","blocks":[{"text":"Black is a color that results from the absence or complete absorption of visible light. It is an achromatic color, without chroma, like white and grey. It is often used symbolically or figuratively to represent darkness. Black and white have often been used to describe opposites such as good and evil, the Dark Ages versus the Age of Enlightenment, and night versus day. Since the Middle Ages, black has been the symbolic color of solemnity and authority, and for this reason it is still commonly worn by judges and magistrates."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Tutankhamun_jackal_%28blacked%29.png/330px-Tutankhamun_jackal_%28blacked%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black","wikidata":"Q23445"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Black_Dahlia_Mugshot.jpg/330px-Black_Dahlia_Mugshot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Black_Dahlia_Mugshot.jpg/330px-Black_Dahlia_Mugshot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Short's arrest photo from 1943 for underage drinking","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlack_Dahlia_Mugshot.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Elizabeth_Short_death_certificate.png/500px-Elizabeth_Short_death_certificate.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Elizabeth_Short_death_certificate.png/500px-Elizabeth_Short_death_certificate.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Short's death certificate","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AElizabeth_Short_death_certificate.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Kingsbury_Run_investigations%2C_sept_1936.jpg/330px-Kingsbury_Run_investigations%2C_sept_1936.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Kingsbury_Run_investigations%2C_sept_1936.jpg/330px-Kingsbury_Run_investigations%2C_sept_1936.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Police search for remains in the Cleveland Torso Murders, 1936; some journalists and law enforcement have speculated a connection between the Cleveland crimes and Short's murder.[h]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKingsbury_Run_investigations%2C_sept_1936.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Lake-ladd-trailer.jpg/500px-Lake-ladd-trailer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Lake-ladd-trailer.jpg/1280px-Lake-ladd-trailer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Lake-ladd-trailer.jpg/500px-Lake-ladd-trailer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Some sources attribute the Black Dahlia name to the 1946 film noir The Blue Dahlia, starring Veronica Lake and Alan Ladd (pictured).[158]","link":"https://en.wikiped